From 029c6ac3139a9ee82060fd46d730a32971301d10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 07:58:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: zoom each Dolby Vision picture to its active
 area (an expert setting, off)

For a 2.35:1 or 2.39:1 screen (constant image height) and for films that change shape from scene
to scene (IMAX scenes, or a 2.39:1 scene windowboxed in a 1.43:1 master), "Zoom to the active area
of Dolby Vision videos" (videoplayer.hadesactiveareazoom, Player > Videos, expert level, off by
default) fits the part of each picture that its RPU marks as active (level 5) to the screen in
place of the whole coded picture, where that shows it larger.

The screen is Kodi's own: the view window, which in full screen is the area set in the video
calibration (where a 2.35:1 or 2.39:1 screen is told, top and bottom), and the pixel ratio of the
resolution (an anamorphic lens). The area is fitted as Kodi fits a picture, whole and as large as
the screen takes it, so nothing of the active picture is ever cut off: a scene taller than the
screen (1.78:1 or 1.90:1 on a 2.39:1 screen) fills the screen's height with bars at its sides. The
whole picture is drawn larger, its bars beyond the screen, as Kodi's own zoom draws a picture: the
source stays the whole picture, so Kodi's drawing, native Dolby Vision through the part of the
picture shown (DVBRIDGE::ViewGeometry()) and its conversion to HDR10 or SDR take it as they take
Kodi's zoom, and the level 5 block sent with native output is that of the picture as drawn
(dvbridge_dv_area(), through the view). On a 2.39:1 screen set in the calibration, a 2.39:1 film in
a 16:9 picture is drawn whole at its own size, the place native output takes on a 16:9 TV. On a
16:9 TV the same film is already as large as it can be, and nothing changes.

What is zoomed to goes by the pictures in their order, as they are queued (CAreaHysteresis, the
player thread), and travels with each picture to the screen:
- a change at a picture that its RPU marks as the first of a scene is taken with that picture;
  marks closer than half a second (an RPU that marks every picture) are no cuts;
- otherwise more of the picture active is taken once two pictures within four bring it (their
  union: a block that alternates is taken whole, and holds), and less of it once it has held for a
  second: a level 5 block that changes for one picture, or wanders in a fade or a dark scene, moves
  nothing, and nothing active is left out for longer;
- less of the picture by at most 1/256 of its size on an edge is no change;
- a start, a seek, or a flush or reset of the decoder (DiscardBuffer(), Flush()) starts it again:
  the first picture after it is taken as it comes.
Without an area that can be used, the whole picture (fail-closed): no Dolby Vision metadata of the
video's own (not those Hades makes of HDR10 or HDR10+), no level 5 block or more than one, offsets
that fit no reading of the picture (dvbridge_l5_area()), an area of less than 40 % of the picture
in width or height, or offsets that the measurement of native output found picture across
(dvbridge_l5check.h; CDVBridgeGLES::L5LeftAside()).

How it is drawn goes by the picture presented (CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea(), the render
thread): only in the view mode Normal as Kodi sets it (a view mode, zoom, pixel ratio, shift or
stretch that the user chose is the user's), not for a 3D or a rotated video, not while the disc is
in its menu (whose graphics are placed on the whole picture), and only where it shows the area
larger. A subtitle in a picture that is a line of text is kept on the screen while it zooms, moved
no further than it takes (KeptInViewOffset()); text subtitles are placed in the screen already. A
line in kodi.log says when the zoom starts, changes or stops, with the area, how it was taken and
why it stops.

With the setting off nothing is read and nothing is placed differently: ManageRenderArea() goes to
Kodi's own CalcNormalRenderRect() with the same values (split into CalcDestRect() and the new
ClipRenderRect(), the same code), and the subtitles are placed as before. A host test covers the
hysteresis, the place and the reading of the level 5 block (TestActiveAreaZoom.cpp).

The idea is CorentinB's ("Dolby Vision active area", a view mode in another build); written anew
here.
